  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=3-5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237395/assets/8e6594c570523f66eaa9b5cce5ea960f438698caa18d1f150055e88518b936b4.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})≈1.030\\,38.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=2\\pi -1.030\\,38≈5.25\n$$\n\nto two decimal places.",
